Removes the white letterbox bars baked into resized Amasty Blog thumbnails.

Problem: Amasty forces the editor to enter a fixed image width/height in the blog
configuration. When an uploaded image's aspect ratio does not match those dimensions,
the generated thumbnail is rendered with white bars on the top/bottom (or sides).

Root cause: Helper\Image::resizeImage() calls keepFrame(true) together with
backgroundColor([255, 255, 255]). keepFrame(true) forces the output image to the exact
configured width x height frame and pads the leftover space with the background colour,
so the white bars are physically baked into the cached image file (CSS object-fit cannot
strip them).

Fix: set keepFrame(false). With keepAspectRatio still active the thumbnail is downscaled
to fit within the configured box while preserving its real aspect ratio (no padding), so
the cached file contains only image pixels. Front-end templates can then crop it cleanly
with object-fit: cover. Downscaling and the resized-image cache are preserved.

diff --git a/Helper/Image.php b/Helper/Image.php
index b05e570..0295f37 100644
--- a/Helper/Image.php
+++ b/Helper/Image.php
@@ -145,7 +145,10 @@ class Image extends AbstractHelper
         $imageResize->backgroundColor([255, 255, 255]);
         $imageResize->constrainOnly(true);
         $imageResize->keepTransparency(true);
-        $imageResize->keepFrame(true);
+        // BEGIN PATCH
+        // $imageResize->keepFrame(true);
+        $imageResize->keepFrame(false);
+        // END PATCH
         if ($this->configProvider->isKeepAspectRatio()) {
             $imageResize->keepAspectRatio(true);
         }
